Let X be an infinite topological space. A countable cover U of X by Borel sets (none of which equals X) is:
• a large cover if every x ∈ X belongs to infinitely many members of U;
• an ω-cover if every finite F ⊆ X is contained in some member of U;
• a τ-cover if it is a large cover and for every x, y ∈ X either {U ∈ U : x ∈ U, y ∉ U} or {U ∈ U : y ∈ U, x ∉ U} is finite;
• a γ-cover if it is infinite and every x ∈ X belongs to all but finitely many members of U.

Denote by BΛ, BΩ, BT, BΓ the families of all countable Borel large, ω-, τ-, and γ-covers of X, respectively. For families U and V of covers, say that X satisfies Split(U, V) if every cover U ∈ U can be partitioned into two disjoint subcovers V₁, V₂ ⊆ U with V₁, V₂ ∈ V.

A property P of topological spaces is hereditary if whenever X has property P, every subspace Y ⊆ X also has property P (with the Borel covers computed in Y).

Question: Is any of the four properties
   Split(BΩ, BΛ),   Split(BΩ, BΩ),   Split(BT, BT),   Split(BT, BΓ)
hereditary?

